# Privacy Policy for Qalam - Recite & Reflect

**Last updated:** 24 April 2026

Qalam - Recite & Reflect ("Qalam", "the app") is a Quran reader developed by Under a Crescent Moon as a single-developer personal project. This policy explains what happens to your data when you use the app. The short version: Qalam does not collect, store, or transmit any personal information about you. Everything the app remembers stays on your device.

## What Qalam does not do

- Qalam does not collect personal information of any kind.
- Qalam does not have user accounts or a login system.
- Qalam does not include analytics, telemetry, crash reporting, or any third-party SDK that sends data off your device.
- Qalam does not show advertisements and does not integrate with any ad network.
- Qalam does not track you across apps or websites.
- Qalam does not sell, share, or disclose information about you to anyone, because it does not have any information about you to share.

## What Qalam stores on your device

The app saves the following entirely on your device, using Android's standard app-private storage. None of it leaves the device:

- Your reading preferences (selected translation, font size, mushaf style, theme, layout choices, and similar settings).
- Your last-read position, so the app can offer to resume where you left off.
- Your bookmarks.
- A local history of reading sessions and recitation playback, used to show you personal statistics such as reading time and streaks on the in-app "Your journey" screen.
- Downloaded recitation audio files, when you choose the "Keep forever" retention option.
- A short-lived cache of streamed recitation audio, used to avoid repeated downloads.

All of the above is accessible only to Qalam on your device. It is deleted when you uninstall the app. You can also reset your statistics and clear downloaded audio from inside the app's Settings screen.

## Network connections Qalam makes

Qalam is almost entirely offline. It bundles the full Quran text, all translations it supports, the transliteration data, tajweed coloring data, and the complete Juz Amma recitation audio directly inside the app.

The one exception is recitation audio for surahs outside Juz Amma. When you play audio that is not bundled, the app streams the MP3 file directly from `everyayah.com`, a public Quran recitation archive. This means your device's IP address is visible to everyayah.com during streaming, in the same way that loading any web page makes your IP address visible to the site you are loading. Qalam sends no other information in these requests. No user identifier, no device identifier, no usage data.

If you never play audio outside Juz Amma, the app makes no network requests at all.

## Permissions Qalam requests

- **Internet access** - used only for streaming recitation audio from everyayah.com, as described above.
- **Foreground service (media playback)** - required by Android to keep recitation playing when the screen is off or the app is in the background.
- **Post notifications** (Android 13 and above) - used only to display the media playback notification while audio is playing, so you can pause or skip from the lock screen. Qalam does not send promotional or engagement notifications.

Qalam does not request access to your contacts, location, camera, microphone, storage of other apps, or any other sensitive permission.

## Children

Qalam is suitable for all ages. Because the app does not collect any personal information from anyone, it does not collect personal information from children. There are no accounts, no social features, no advertising, no in-app purchases, and no links that take a child outside the app to external content, aside from the credits page which attributes the sources of the Quran text, translations, fonts, and recitation.

## Data sources and attribution

The Arabic Quran text, translations, transliteration, tajweed annotations, fonts, and recitation audio bundled in Qalam come from publicly available sources with appropriate licenses. These sources are listed in the app's Settings > Credits screen. Using the app does not send any information to those sources. Bundled content has already been downloaded and packaged with the app itself.

